{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"noexcept-exception-safety","title":"noexcept & Exception Safety","group":"Errors, Streams & Utilities","order":64,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>noexcept</code> promises a function won't throw — enabling optimisations like moving elements during <code>vector</code> growth. Exception-safety guarantees describe what state is left behind when something <em>does</em> throw.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield\"></i><code>noexcept</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;type_traits&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint safeAdd(int a, int b) noexcept { return a + b; }\nvoid mayThrow() { throw 1; }\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nvoid swapValues(T&amp; a, T&amp; b) noexcept(std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v&lt;T&gt;) {   // conditional\n    T tmp = std::move(a);\n    a = std::move(b);\n    b = std::move(tmp);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::boolalpha\n              &lt;&lt; noexcept(safeAdd(1, 2)) &lt;&lt; ' '          // noexcept operator: compile-time query\n              &lt;&lt; noexcept(mayThrow()) &lt;&lt; ' '\n              &lt;&lt; std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v&lt;std::string&gt; &lt;&lt; ' '\n              &lt;&lt; std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v&lt;std::vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::string a = \"x\", b = \"y\";\n    swapValues(a, b);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true false true true\nyx</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — throwing from noexcept terminates</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\nvoid promise() noexcept {\n    throw std::runtime_error(\"broken promise\");   // compiles, but calls std::terminate at run time\n}\nint main() { promise(); }</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>Destructors, <code>operator delete</code> and (by default) compiler-generated move operations of well-behaved members are <code>noexcept</code>.</li><li>Mark move constructors/assignments and <code>swap</code> <code>noexcept</code> — containers depend on it.</li><li>Don't mark functions <code>noexcept</code> if they might throw someday; removing it later breaks callers that relied on it.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stairs\"></i>The exception-safety guarantees</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Guarantee</th><th>Promise if an exception is thrown</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>No-throw</strong></td><td>Never throws</td><td>Destructors, <code>swap</code>, move operations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Strong</strong></td><td>Commit-or-rollback: state is unchanged</td><td><code>vector::push_back</code> (when the move ctor is <code>noexcept</code> or copyable)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Basic</strong></td><td>No leaks, invariants hold, but state may have changed</td><td>Most operations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>None</strong></td><td>Anything goes — avoid</td><td>Code with raw <code>new</code>/<code>delete</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Getting the strong guarantee: copy-and-swap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nclass Inventory {\n    std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; items_;\npublic:\n    void addAll(const std::vector&lt;std::string&gt;&amp; newItems) {\n        std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; copy = items_;          // 1. work on a copy\n        for (const auto&amp; it : newItems) {\n            if (it.empty()) throw std::invalid_argument(\"empty item\");\n            copy.push_back(it);\n        }\n        items_.swap(copy);                               // 2. commit with a no-throw swap\n    }\n    std::size_t size() const { return items_.size(); }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Inventory inv;\n    inv.addAll({\"pen\", \"ink\"});\n    try { inv.addAll({\"cap\", \"\", \"pad\"}); } catch (const std::exception&amp; e) { std::cout &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; inv.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';                      // still 2: nothing half-added\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>empty item\n2</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why are destructors implicitly noexcept?</summary><div><p>Destructors run during stack unwinding. If one threw while another exception was already propagating, C++ couldn't handle two exceptions at once and would call <code>std::terminate</code>. Making them <code>noexcept</code> by default turns that into a clear rule: never let exceptions escape a destructor.</p></div></details></section>"}